# The UP-Term window
> Some things you do in the UP-Term window itself, not at the shell: tabs, menus, slash
> commands, and the mouse. The window counts them, and this lesson reads the counts with
> `UPTerm state`, so it can tell that you really did each one. For these steps you do the
> action, then press Return at the tutorial prompt with nothing typed.
$ tabs0=$(${UPTERM_STATE:-UPTerm} state tabs-opened)
? Open a new tab with Right Amiga T, then press Return here (in this tab).
= [ "$(${UPTERM_STATE:-UPTerm} state tabs-opened)" -gt "$tabs0" ]
~ Hold Right Amiga and press T; the new tab opens. Come back to this tab and press Return.
! echo "Press Right Amiga T, then Return."; ${UPTERM_STATE:+$UPTERM_STATE act tabs-opened}
> Tabs are shells side by side. The tab bar shows them; click one, or press Right Amiga
> with its number (1 is the first tab), to switch.
$ sw0=$(${UPTERM_STATE:-UPTerm} state tab-switches)
? Switch to the other tab and back to this one, then press Return.
= [ "$(${UPTERM_STATE:-UPTerm} state tab-switches)" -gt "$sw0" ]
~ Click the other tab in the tab bar, or press Right Amiga 1 and Right Amiga 2, and end in this tab.
! echo "Switch tabs, then Return."; ${UPTERM_STATE:+$UPTERM_STATE act tab-switches}
> The menus hold the same actions, and the Help menu explains the terminal.
$ menu0=$(${UPTERM_STATE:-UPTerm} state menu-picks)
? Open the Help menu and pick Slash commands, then press Return.
= [ "$(${UPTERM_STATE:-UPTerm} state menu-picks)" -gt "$menu0" ]
~ Hold the right mouse button on the title bar, move to Help, release on Slash commands.
! echo "Pick Help, Slash commands, then Return."; ${UPTERM_STATE:+$UPTERM_STATE act menu-picks}
> A line that starts with a slash is a command for the window, not for the shell. The
> window answers it and the shell sees an empty line, so just press Return after it.
$ slash0=$(${UPTERM_STATE:-UPTerm} state slash-count)
? Type /help and press Return.
= [ "$(${UPTERM_STATE:-UPTerm} state slash-count)" -gt "$slash0" ]
= [ "$(${UPTERM_STATE:-UPTerm} state last-slash)" = help ]
~ Type a slash and the word help, then Return.
! echo "Type /help."; ${UPTERM_STATE:+$UPTERM_STATE act slash help}
> Copy takes text with the mouse: drag over a word and it is copied at once.
$ LEARN_WORD=up$$
$ copies0=$(${UPTERM_STATE:-UPTerm} state copies)
? Drag the mouse over this word to copy it, then press Return: @WORD@
= [ "$(${UPTERM_STATE:-UPTerm} state copies)" -gt "$copies0" ]
~ Press the left mouse button at the start of the word, drag to its end, release.
! echo "Drag over the word, then Return."; ${UPTERM_STATE:+$UPTERM_STATE act copies}
? Type  echo  and a space, paste the word (Right Amiga V), and press Return.
= grep -qx "$LEARN_WORD" "$LEARN_OUT"
~ Type echo and a space, press Right Amiga V, then Return. echo prints the word you copied.
! echo $LEARN_WORD
> Right Amiga with a menu item's letter picks the item without the menu. Clear screen is K.
$ key0=$(${UPTERM_STATE:-UPTerm} state menu-picks)
? Press Right Amiga K to clear the screen, then press Return.
= [ "$(${UPTERM_STATE:-UPTerm} state menu-picks)" -gt "$key0" ]
~ Hold Right Amiga and press K, then Return.
! echo "Press Right Amiga K, then Return."; ${UPTERM_STATE:+$UPTERM_STATE act menu-picks}
